o be that gold treats manifestations reported as the patient's actual diagnoses (even generic-sounding ones) as SpecificDisease, while the model is over-applying a class label to terms used diagnostically rather than categorically. This looks less like a clean linguistic rule and more like an annotation-consistency quirk in how the gold set treats diagnosis-in-context versus taxonomic category.\n\nLanding on a clearer distinction: generic pathology-noun mentions (vasculitis, arthritis, infection) read as superordinate category labels become SpecificDisease when framed as an actual diagnosis the patient has or is being differentiated against, while DiseaseClass stays reserved for genuinely generic descriptive traits like \"mental retardation\" without a concrete diagnostic claim attached.\n\nThe real contrast is that TP DiseaseClass terms like \"mental retardation\" are broad nonspecific clinical descriptors, while TP SpecificDisease mentions are proper names, eponyms, or etiologically precise entities. So the rule should be: before labeling a pathology-category noun as DiseaseClass, check whether it has a proper name/eponym, a qualifier pinning a single organism or etiology, or is treated as a concrete diagnosis this patient actually has — any of these push it toward SpecificDisease instead.\n\nBut testing \"arthritis\" against this rule is tricky — it's a presenting symptom alongside fever and vasculitis, not the diagnosis itself, which is chronic meningococcemia. So it doesn't cleanly satisfy any of the tests, suggesting the gold labeling may be treating patient-level named conditions as SpecificDisease while reserving DiseaseClass strictly for heterogeneous umbrella or trait terms, which I should flag as a residual ambiguity rather than a clean rule.\n\nI'm also looking at \"Chronic neisserial infection\" in the same document, where \"neisserial\" functions as a genus adjective and \"infection\" as a generic process noun, which could lead the model to treat \"inf